# wip — interpretation-trials
# Started: 2026-11-06

#>plan_interpretation_trials
#:plan #!public_interest_research
#:layer #!semantics

?["card"]{
 phase: #seed
 gist: "Open readings of Spw's marks held as trials, each scored expressive, interactive and grounded before it becomes grammar"
 touches: [~".agents/plans/interpretation-trials"]
 entry: ~"./interpretation-trials.spw"
 next: "The owner picks a card when ready; ^reach orders the cards by lines moved, and npm run analyze:trials shows seed's two readers side by side on any card."
}

^["edges"]{
 feeds: [~"../brace-slot-reading/wip.spw", ~"../syntax-hardware-alignment/wip.spw", ~"../inquiry-loop/wip.spw", ~"../ground-edge-reading/wip.spw", ~"../relationship-contours/wip.spw", ~"../onboarding-quest/wip.spw"]
 shares: [~"../reading-drift/wip.spw", ~"../editor-inspection-instruments/wip.spw", ~"../plan-ecology-clustering/wip.spw"]
}

^["roots"]{
 @plan: ~".agents/plans/interpretation-trials/PLAN.md"
 @trials: ~".agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw"
 @brainstorm: ~".spw/caches/journal/2026-11-06-reading-brainstorm.spw"
 @progressive: ~"packages/spw-seed/src/ir/progressive.ts"
}

^["intent"]{
 ~#goal: "Spw grows toward novel interactive semantics, and each step rests on something linguistically expressive (the owner, 2026-11-06)"
 ~#taste: "Try readings on purpose before deciding them; keep a reading when it lets a writer say more, lets a host do more, and mirrors how people already mean"
}

^["reach"]{
 # Lines each card's answer would re-read, as counted so far; an order for the owner's hours, not a verdict. Counts cite their source.
 spaced_equals: .{ here: 2174, site_consumer: 25429, counts: "lines opening key = value", source: "syntax-hardware-alignment p13, 2026-11-06" }
 register: .{ here: 916, site_consumer: 6890, counts: "[reg=…] stamps; 832 and 6,719 restate their container", source: "syntax-hardware-alignment stream, 2026-10-27 and 2026-11-08" }
 dot: .{ here: 33, site_consumer: 564, second_consumer: 173, counts: "single-line bodies joined by a dot; 241 of the 381 bodies in the site's expression lines", source: "journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement" }
 angle_readings: .{ here: 10, site_consumer: 347, counts: "lines ending in a <tag> that takes the next line's head", source: "the owner's reading notes, 2026-11-06" }
 gap: .{ here: 5, site_consumer: 3, second_consumer: 230, counts: "single-line bodies with terms side by side", source: "journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement" }
 slot_order: .{ site_consumer: 0, counts: "typed expression lines, of 387, that depart from word, mode, body, scene, concept", source: "journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement" }
 reading: "By lines moved, the spaced = and the register come first on the site, and the dot decides its expression lines; the gap is one consumer's dialect; slot order moves no typed line yet. Cards with no count here have not been measured."
}

^["open"]{
 # Unresolved questions. When resolved: log in stream, then move to ^["done"] as ~#resolved(label).
 ?[first_trial]: "Which card runs first? The spaced = and the <…> readings move the most lines; the stages and guided-wonder cards move the most hosts. ^reach now holds the counts: the spaced = (25,429 site lines), the register (6,890 stamps) and the dot (564 bodies) lead."
 ?[bench_readers]: "Does the bench import the consumer's slot reader and factshift.center's line anatomy by path, read-only, or do those readers print their readings for the bench to collect?"
 ?[novice_trials]: "Are trial cards also quest material: can a newcomer run one, judge it, and leave an ask?"
}

^["commits"]{
 # Named, in order. Planned ~[name]; landed .[name](hash). Names are never reused.
 .[trial_bench](56d6245d): "^seed[analyzers] — read every form on a trial card through each reader and print the readings side by side, with the card's holes left open; scripts/analyzers/spw-trial-bench.ts reads the cards with the parser and each form with the parser and the line reader, and says where they part (trial.bench/1; npm run analyze:trials)"
 ~[trial_bench_host_readers]: "^seed[analyzers] — the bench gains the consumer readers as columns, after ?[bench_readers]"
 ~[trial_bench_pairs]: "^seed[analyzers] — the bench reads each card's ~#pairs as two forms and prints the first place their readings differ, per reader"
 ~[first_trials]: ".[plans] — the owner runs the first trials and fills judgment and answer; each card keeps its ~#expect, written before the run"
 ~[promote_kept_readings]: ".[plans] — a reading kept on all three axes moves to its home plan as a decision citing its trial; a reading dropped stays on the card with why"
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] decide — The owner asked to record the day's lines of wonder and to test Spw's interpretations and utilities more intentionally later, and set a direction: novel interactive semantics, grounded in something linguistically expressive. Opened this plan to hold sixteen trial cards with the owner's feature-card method and a rubric: expressive, interactive, grounded, cost.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] observe — The readers already disagree on the same lines. Seed reads <person>home as two expressions where factshift.center reads a concept written first, and reads ecestudent@ece.120~?[transistors work.how] as four expressions where factshift.center reads two lines tied by @ and ~ bonds. factshift.center reads lines with its own slot reader (line-anatomy.ts) and carries an older workbench copy (fffab72, 2026-03-12) under its app.
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] note — Two cards came from the owner mid-pass: guided wonder (a newcomer's imprecise wonder guided by constructs in existing scripts; grounded in recasts and construction grammar) and stages (one script on several canvases along a projection gradient; the workbench's source products tokens, structure and trace are the first rungs, and factshift.center's panels already read in three levels).
 >>[2026-11-06 18:10] note — Added wonder_trial_hash_format: how a slot hash is written (positional, a #-led name, or line-shaped). slotHash ships in seed as a primitive with no written form, at the owner's choice (2026-11-06), so a canvas host can try it for first layout and hue before the form is decided.
 >>[2026-11-06 20:20] note — Added wonder_trial_prime_actualize: both hosts now act in two steps (spw.quest primes an operator before it actualizes; factshift.center's tools prime and actualize), which may be Spw's own ~ potential and ! action, so a host's act history could be written as Spw.
 >>[2026-11-06 20:28] note — The owner decided that every pair may lead the word (2026-11-06), so wonder_trial_slot_order no longer asks whether order is allowed, only what it says: noise, voice, or information structure. A slot query is to be read as a line (the owner's leaning), which makes wonder_trial_typed_hole a question about what a hole reads as inside a line.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:47] note — Added seven cards (now 25): observer_postfix, clause_authority and comparison from a second mounted consumer's proposals for provenance and auditable computation (its folio spell degrades today on <=); sigil_role and valence_word from its review and from the site's plane reports (2026-11-07), which find three readers parting on the dot, on < >, on operator names and on land sigils; dot from those reports; intermediate_forms from the owner. unread_field gains the consumer's header lenses that no tool reads.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:47] note — wonder_trial_intermediate_forms holds the owner's wonder (2026-11-08) about Spw as a philosophical instrument for creativity and focused attention: intermediate forms, examples and component physics that invite generative prompting and query writing in Spw's own constructs. Kin to stages (a projection gradient) and guided_wonder (exemplars from mounted scripts); its interactive column leans on line-kit line_features and slot_query, and on factshift.center's circling options.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:47] observe — The site's plane reports add readers to angle_readings: within the site, < > is what this is about on the guide and where it goes on /forms/ and /pivot, so the site's own readers part before seed is asked.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:56] note — Added wonder_trial_register (26 cards): six readings of one word, from the owner's remark that registers could be made more useful. Normal form already uses registers to record how a node was built, which is the intermediate-forms card's exemplar reading in miniature: a middle form that remembers its source.
 >>[2026-11-08 10:07] note — Added wonder_trial_rest_state and wonder_trial_play_schemas (28 cards), from docs/theory/spw/exhibit-floor.spw: the owner's high-school volunteering on a children's museum floor (resetting exhibits, watching children play, trying the physics, noticing light, texture and art) read as four vantages on interactive models. The intermediate_forms card's physics reading now has its conditions written there: immediate, consistent, reversible, conserved, legible, open-ended.
 >>[2026-11-09 17:07] note — Added wonder_trial_gap and wonder_trial_shape (30 cards), from a run of both seed readers over three corpora (journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement). The gap card asks whether terms side by side are company, an order, one phrase or unread; grounded in asyndeton. The shape card asks whether a line with its words taken out is still a line; grounded in argument-structure constructions, and written with _ so a shape is a query. The dot card now records that seed holds two readers that part on it, and the slot-order card that no typed line in the site consumer departs from one order.
 >>[2026-11-09 17:11] implement — trial_bench is written for seed readers: scripts/analyzers/spw-trial-bench.ts reads the cards through the parser (an anchor, a ? frame, its forms) and each form through the parser and the line reader, then names where they part: on line count, on body part count, or on the marks parts lead with. First run over 30 cards and 94 forms: the two readers part on 54 (40 on lines, 13 on parts, 1 on marks). The lines figure is rough, since some forms are prose the line reader never claimed; parts and marks are exact. Host reader columns wait on bench_readers, and a pairs runner is named as the next slice.
}

^["cache"]{
 ~#status: "seed"
